1
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:07,719
The apparition of a file of soldiers ringing down the but-ends of their

2
00:00:07,719 --> 00:00:13,440
loaded muskets on our door-step, caused the dinner-party to rise from table in confusion,

3
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:18,559
and caused Mrs. Joe re-entering the kitchen empty-handed, to stop short and stare,

4
00:00:18,559 --> 00:00:26,400
in her wondering lament of “Gracious goodness gracious me, what’s gone—with the—pie!”

5
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:31,519
The sergeant and I were in the kitchen when Mrs. Joe stood staring; at which crisis I

6
00:00:31,519 --> 00:00:37,320
partially recovered the use of my senses. It was the sergeant who had spoken to me,

7
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:40,079
and he was now looking round at the company, with his handcuffs

8
00:00:40,079 --> 00:00:46,079
invitingly extended towards them in his right hand, and his left on my shoulder.

9
00:00:46,079 --> 00:00:49,280
“Excuse me, ladies and gentleman,” said the sergeant, “but as I have

10
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:53,639
mentioned at the door to this smart young shaver,” (which he hadn’t),

11
00:00:53,639 --> 00:00:57,440
“I am on a chase in the name of the king, and I want the blacksmith.”

12
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:01,039
“And pray what might you want with him?” retorted my sister,

13
00:01:01,039 --> 00:01:04,039
quick to resent his being wanted at all.

14
00:01:04,039 --> 00:01:10,360
“Missis,” returned the gallant sergeant, “speaking for myself, I should reply, the honour and

15
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:17,400
pleasure of his fine wife’s acquaintance; speaking for the king, I answer, a little job done.”

16
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,559
This was received as rather neat in the sergeant;

17
00:01:20,559 --> 00:01:25,800
insomuch that Mr. Pumblechook cried audibly, “Good again!”

18
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:31,840
“You see, blacksmith,” said the sergeant, who had by this time picked out Joe with his eye, “we have

19
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:37,000
had an accident with these, and I find the lock of one of ’em goes wrong, and the coupling don’t

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,440
act pretty. As they are wanted for immediate service, will you throw your eye over them?”

21
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:48,039
Joe threw his eye over them, and pronounced that the job would necessitate the lighting of his

22
00:01:48,039 --> 00:01:56,440
forge fire, and would take nearer two hours than one. “Will it? Then will you set about it at once,

23
00:01:56,440 --> 00:02:02,159
blacksmith?” said the off-hand sergeant, “as it’s on his Majesty’s service. And if

24
00:02:02,159 --> 00:02:05,639
my men can bear a hand anywhere, they’ll make themselves useful.”

25
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:11,519
With that, he called to his men, who came trooping into the kitchen one after another,

26
00:02:11,519 --> 00:02:17,440
and piled their arms in a corner. And then they stood about, as soldiers do; now,

27
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:24,199
with their hands loosely clasped before them; now, resting a knee or a shoulder; now, easing

28
00:02:24,199 --> 00:02:32,000
a belt or a pouch; now, opening the door to spit stiffly over their high stocks, out into the yard.

29
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,960
All these things I saw without then knowing that I saw them,

30
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:41,000
for I was in an agony of apprehension. But beginning to perceive that the handcuffs

31
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,719
were not for me, and that the military had so far got the better of the pie as to put

32
00:02:45,719 --> 00:02:51,119
it in the background, I collected a little more of my scattered wits.

33
00:02:51,119 --> 00:02:55,800
“Would you give me the time?” said the sergeant, addressing himself to Mr. Pumblechook,

34
00:02:55,800 --> 00:03:01,719
as to a man whose appreciative powers justified the inference that he was equal to the time.

35
00:03:01,719 --> 00:03:04,360
“It’s just gone half past two.”

36
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:07,559
“That’s not so bad,” said the sergeant, reflecting;

37
00:03:07,559 --> 00:03:11,440
“even if I was forced to halt here nigh two hours, that’ll do. How far

38
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:16,239
might you call yourselves from the marshes, hereabouts? Not above a mile, I reckon?”

39
00:03:16,239 --> 00:03:18,679
“Just a mile,” said Mrs. Joe.

40
00:03:18,679 --> 00:03:23,159
“That’ll do. We begin to close in upon ’em about dusk. A little before dusk,

41
00:03:23,159 --> 00:03:25,719
my orders are. That’ll do.”

42
00:03:25,719 --> 00:03:30,400
“Convicts, sergeant?” asked Mr. Wopsle, in a matter-of-course way.

43
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:35,760
“Ay!” returned the sergeant, “two. They’re pretty well known to be out on the marshes still, and

44
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:41,800
they won’t try to get clear of ’em before dusk. Anybody here seen anything of any such game?”

45
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:47,800
Everybody, myself excepted, said no, with confidence. Nobody thought of me.

46
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:52,199
“Well!” said the sergeant, “they’ll find themselves trapped in a circle, I expect,

47
00:03:52,199 --> 00:03:59,559
sooner than they count on. Now, blacksmith! If you’re ready, his Majesty the King is.”

48
00:03:59,559 --> 00:04:03,480
Joe had got his coat and waistcoat and cravat off, and his leather apron on,

49
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:08,280
and passed into the forge. One of the soldiers opened its wooden windows,

50
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:13,719
another lighted the fire, another turned to at the bellows, the rest stood round the blaze,

51
00:04:13,719 --> 00:04:20,880
which was soon roaring. Then Joe began to hammer and clink, hammer and clink, and we all looked on.

52
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:25,159
The interest of the impending pursuit not only absorbed the general attention,

53
00:04:25,159 --> 00:04:30,840
but even made my sister liberal. She drew a pitcher of beer from the cask for the soldiers,

54
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:35,440
and invited the sergeant to take a glass of brandy. But Mr. Pumblechook said, sharply,

55
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:41,360
“Give him wine, Mum. I’ll engage there’s no tar in that:” so, the sergeant thanked

56
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:45,800
him and said that as he preferred his drink without tar, he would take wine,

57
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:50,679
if it was equally convenient. When it was given him, he drank his Majesty’s health

58
00:04:50,679 --> 00:04:56,119
and compliments of the season, and took it all at a mouthful and smacked his lips.

59
00:04:56,119 --> 00:05:00,199
“Good stuff, eh, sergeant?” said Mr. Pumblechook.

60
00:05:00,199 --> 00:05:05,439
“I’ll tell you something,” returned the sergeant; “I suspect that stuff’s of your providing.”

61
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:12,199
Mr. Pumblechook, with a fat sort of laugh, said, “Ay, ay? Why?”

62
00:05:12,199 --> 00:05:16,079
“Because,” returned the sergeant, clapping him on the shoulder,

63
00:05:16,079 --> 00:05:18,520
“you’re a man that knows what’s what.”

64
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:26,039
“D’ye think so?” said Mr. Pumblechook, with his former laugh. “Have another glass!”

65
00:05:26,039 --> 00:05:31,720
“With you. Hob and nob,” returned the sergeant. “The top of mine to the foot

66
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:37,680
of yours,—the foot of yours to the top of mine,—Ring once, ring twice,—the

67
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:42,079
best tune on the Musical Glasses! Your health. May you live a thousand years,

68
00:05:42,079 --> 00:05:48,199
and never be a worse judge of the right sort than you are at the present moment of your life!”

69
00:05:48,199 --> 00:05:53,720
The sergeant tossed off his glass again and seemed quite ready for another glass. I noticed

70
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:58,840
that Mr. Pumblechook in his hospitality appeared to forget that he had made a present of the wine,

71
00:05:58,840 --> 00:06:04,760
but took the bottle from Mrs. Joe and had all the credit of handing it about in a gush of joviality.

72
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:11,319
Even I got some. And he was so very free of the wine that he even called for the other bottle,

73
00:06:11,319 --> 00:06:17,279
and handed that about with the same liberality, when the first was gone.

74
00:06:17,279 --> 00:06:23,720
As I watched them while they all stood clustering about the forge, enjoying themselves so much,

75
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:29,800
I thought what terrible good sauce for a dinner my fugitive friend on the marshes was. They had not

76
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:33,880
enjoyed themselves a quarter so much, before the entertainment was brightened with the

77
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:40,039
excitement he furnished. And now, when they were all in lively anticipation of “the two villains”

78
00:06:40,039 --> 00:06:46,039
being taken, and when the bellows seemed to roar for the fugitives, the fire to flare for them,

79
00:06:46,039 --> 00:06:51,159
the smoke to hurry away in pursuit of them, Joe to hammer and clink for them,

80
00:06:51,159 --> 00:06:57,560
and all the murky shadows on the wall to shake at them in menace as the blaze rose and sank,

81
00:06:57,560 --> 00:07:03,279
and the red-hot sparks dropped and died, the pale afternoon outside almost seemed

82
00:07:03,279 --> 00:07:09,560
in my pitying young fancy to have turned pale on their account, poor wretches.

83
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:16,399
At last, Joe’s job was done, and the ringing and roaring stopped. As Joe got on his coat, he

84
00:07:16,399 --> 00:07:21,279
mustered courage to propose that some of us should go down with the soldiers and see what came of the

85
00:07:21,279 --> 00:07:28,199
hunt. Mr. Pumblechook and Mr. Hubble declined, on the plea of a pipe and ladies’ society;

86
00:07:28,199 --> 00:07:35,399
but Mr. Wopsle said he would go, if Joe would. Joe said he was agreeable, and would take me, if Mrs.

87
00:07:35,399 --> 00:07:42,039
Joe approved. We never should have got leave to go, I am sure, but for Mrs. Joe’s curiosity to

88
00:07:42,039 --> 00:07:48,439
know all about it and how it ended. As it was, she merely stipulated, “If you bring the boy

89
00:07:48,439 --> 00:07:54,479
back with his head blown to bits by a musket, don’t look to me to put it together again.”

90
00:07:54,479 --> 00:08:00,359
The sergeant took a polite leave of the ladies, and parted from Mr. Pumblechook as from a comrade;

91
00:08:00,359 --> 00:08:05,319
though I doubt if he were quite as fully sensible of that gentleman’s merits under arid conditions,

92
00:08:05,319 --> 00:08:12,239
as when something moist was going. His men resumed their muskets and fell in. Mr. Wopsle,

93
00:08:12,239 --> 00:08:17,000
Joe, and I, received strict charge to keep in the rear, and to speak no word after we reached

94
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:23,000
the marshes. When we were all out in the raw air and were steadily moving towards our business,

95
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:30,119
I treasonably whispered to Joe, “I hope, Joe, we shan’t find them.” and Joe whispered to me,

96
00:08:30,119 --> 00:08:34,560
“I’d give a shilling if they had cut and run, Pip.”

97
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:40,000
We were joined by no stragglers from the village, for the weather was cold and threatening, the way

98
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:45,159
dreary, the footing bad, darkness coming on, and the people had good fires in-doors and were

99
00:08:45,159 --> 00:08:51,880
keeping the day. A few faces hurried to glowing windows and looked after us, but none came out.

100
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:57,239
We passed the finger-post, and held straight on to the churchyard. There we were stopped a

101
00:08:57,239 --> 00:09:02,279
few minutes by a signal from the sergeant’s hand, while two or three of his men dispersed themselves

102
00:09:02,279 --> 00:09:09,079
among the graves, and also examined the porch. They came in again without finding anything,

103
00:09:09,079 --> 00:09:15,279
and then we struck out on the open marshes, through the gate at the side of the churchyard.

104
00:09:15,279 --> 00:09:21,479
A bitter sleet came rattling against us here on the east wind, and Joe took me on his back.

105
00:09:21,479 --> 00:09:25,800
Now that we were out upon the dismal wilderness where they little thought I had been within

106
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:31,119
eight or nine hours and had seen both men hiding, I considered for the first time,

107
00:09:31,119 --> 00:09:35,399
with great dread, if we should come upon them, would my particular convict

108
00:09:35,399 --> 00:09:42,560
suppose that it was I who had brought the soldiers there? He had asked me if I was a deceiving imp,

109
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:46,960
and he had said I should be a fierce young hound if I joined the hunt against him. Would

110
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:52,399
he believe that I was both imp and hound in treacherous earnest, and had betrayed him?

111
00:09:52,399 --> 00:09:57,760
It was of no use asking myself this question now. There I was, on Joe’s back,

112
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:03,439
and there was Joe beneath me, charging at the ditches like a hunter, and stimulating Mr. Wopsle

113
00:10:03,439 --> 00:10:09,439
not to tumble on his Roman nose, and to keep up with us. The soldiers were in front of us,

114
00:10:09,439 --> 00:10:15,840
extending into a pretty wide line with an interval between man and man. We were taking the course I

115
00:10:15,840 --> 00:10:22,079
had begun with, and from which I had diverged in the mist. Either the mist was not out again yet,

116
00:10:22,079 --> 00:10:29,840
or the wind had dispelled it. Under the low red glare of sunset, the beacon, and the gibbet,

117
00:10:29,840 --> 00:10:33,000
and the mound of the Battery, and the opposite shore of the river,

118
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:39,720
were plain, though all of a watery lead colour. With my heart thumping like a blacksmith at Joe’s

119
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:45,319
broad shoulder, I looked all about for any sign of the convicts. I could see none, I could hear

120
00:10:45,319 --> 00:10:52,800
none. Mr. Wopsle had greatly alarmed me more than once, by his blowing and hard breathing;

121
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:58,720
but I knew the sounds by this time, and could dissociate them from the object of pursuit. I

122
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:04,880
got a dreadful start, when I thought I heard the file still going; but it was only a sheep-bell.

123
00:11:04,880 --> 00:11:09,600
The sheep stopped in their eating and looked timidly at us; and the cattle, their heads

124
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:16,359
turned from the wind and sleet, stared angrily as if they held us responsible for both annoyances;

125
00:11:16,359 --> 00:11:21,880
but, except these things, and the shudder of the dying day in every blade of grass, there was no

126
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:27,319
break in the bleak stillness of the marshes. The soldiers were moving on in the direction

127
00:11:27,319 --> 00:11:33,199
of the old Battery, and we were moving on a little way behind them, when, all of a sudden,

128
00:11:33,199 --> 00:11:39,319
we all stopped. For there had reached us on the wings of the wind and rain, a long shout.

129
00:11:39,319 --> 00:11:46,600
It was repeated. It was at a distance towards the east, but it was long and loud. Nay, there seemed

130
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:53,119
to be two or more shouts raised together,—if one might judge from a confusion in the sound.

131
00:11:53,119 --> 00:11:57,600
To this effect the sergeant and the nearest men were speaking under their breath,

132
00:11:57,600 --> 00:12:06,239
when Joe and I came up. After another moment’s listening, Joe (who was a good judge) agreed,

133
00:12:06,239 --> 00:12:13,760
and Mr. Wopsle (who was a bad judge) agreed. The sergeant, a decisive man,

134
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:19,119
ordered that the sound should not be answered, but that the course should be changed, and that

135
00:12:19,119 --> 00:12:26,319
his men should make towards it “at the double.” So we slanted to the right (where the East was),

136
00:12:26,319 --> 00:12:32,640
and Joe pounded away so wonderfully, that I had to hold on tight to keep my seat.

137
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:38,520
It was a run indeed now, and what Joe called, in the only two words he spoke all the time,

138
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:44,119
“a Winder.” Down banks and up banks, and over gates, and splashing into dikes,

139
00:12:44,119 --> 00:12:51,680
and breaking among coarse rushes: no man cared where he went. As we came nearer to the shouting,

140
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:56,680
it became more and more apparent that it was made by more than one voice. Sometimes, it seemed to

141
00:12:56,680 --> 00:13:02,279
stop altogether, and then the soldiers stopped. When it broke out again, the soldiers made for

142
00:13:02,279 --> 00:13:08,279
it at a greater rate than ever, and we after them. After a while, we had so run it down,

143
00:13:08,279 --> 00:13:15,600
that we could hear one voice calling “Murder!” and another voice, “Convicts! Runaways! Guard! This

144
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:21,960
way for the runaway convicts!” Then both voices would seem to be stifled in a struggle, and then

145
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:29,399
would break out again. And when it had come to this, the soldiers ran like deer, and Joe too.

146
00:13:29,399 --> 00:13:35,119
The sergeant ran in first, when we had run the noise quite down, and two of his men ran in close

147
00:13:35,119 --> 00:13:39,800
upon him. Their pieces were cocked and levelled when we all ran in.

148
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:44,439
“Here are both men!” panted the sergeant, struggling at the bottom of a ditch. “Surrender,

149
00:13:44,439 --> 00:13:49,600
you two! and confound you for two wild beasts! Come asunder!”

150
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:53,520
Water was splashing, and mud was flying, and oaths were being sworn,

151
00:13:53,520 --> 00:13:58,199
and blows were being struck, when some more men went down into the ditch to help the sergeant,

152
00:13:58,199 --> 00:14:03,119
and dragged out, separately, my convict and the other one. Both were bleeding

153
00:14:03,119 --> 00:14:08,319
and panting and execrating and struggling; but of course I knew them both directly.

154
00:14:08,319 --> 00:14:12,680
“Mind!” said my convict, wiping blood from his face with his ragged sleeves,

155
00:14:12,680 --> 00:14:19,079
and shaking torn hair from his fingers: “I took him! I give him up to you! Mind that!”

156
00:14:19,079 --> 00:14:22,560
“It’s not much to be particular about,” said the sergeant;

157
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:27,600
“it’ll do you small good, my man, being in the same plight yourself. Handcuffs there!”

158
00:14:27,600 --> 00:14:31,640
“I don’t expect it to do me any good. I don’t want it to do me more good than it

159
00:14:31,640 --> 00:14:39,039
does now,” said my convict, with a greedy laugh. “I took him. He knows it. That’s enough for me.”

160
00:14:39,039 --> 00:14:44,399
The other convict was livid to look at, and, in addition to the old bruised left side of his face,

161
00:14:44,399 --> 00:14:50,520
seemed to be bruised and torn all over. He could not so much as get his breath to speak, until they

162
00:14:50,520 --> 00:14:56,359
were both separately handcuffed, but leaned upon a soldier to keep himself from falling.

163
00:14:56,359 --> 00:15:02,680
“Take notice, guard,—he tried to murder me,” were his first words.

164
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:09,760
“Tried to murder him?” said my convict, disdainfully. “Try, and not do it? I took him,

165
00:15:09,760 --> 00:15:14,640
and giv’ him up; that’s what I done. I not only prevented him getting off the marshes,

166
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:19,640
but I dragged him here,—dragged him this far on his way back. He’s a gentleman,

167
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:25,359
if you please, this villain. Now, the Hulks has got its gentleman again, through me. Murder

168
00:15:25,359 --> 00:15:30,640
him? Worth my while, too, to murder him, when I could do worse and drag him back!”

169
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:38,399
The other one still gasped, “He tried—he tried-to—murder me. Bear—bear witness.”

170
00:15:38,399 --> 00:15:43,560
“Lookee here!” said my convict to the sergeant. “Single-handed I got clear of the prison-ship;

171
00:15:43,560 --> 00:15:49,840
I made a dash and I done it. I could ha’ got clear of these death-cold flats likewise—look at my leg:

172
00:15:49,840 --> 00:15:53,760
you won’t find much iron on it—if I hadn’t made the discovery that he was

173
00:15:53,760 --> 00:16:00,000
here. Let him go free? Let him profit by the means as I found out? Let him make a

174
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:06,600
tool of me afresh and again? Once more? No, no, no. If I had died at the bottom

175
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:12,000
there,” and he made an emphatic swing at the ditch with his manacled hands,

176
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:17,680
“I’d have held to him with that grip, that you should have been safe to find him in my hold.”

177
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:22,319
The other fugitive, who was evidently in extreme horror of his companion, repeated,

178
00:16:22,319 --> 00:16:26,520
“He tried to murder me. I should have been a dead man if you had not come up.”

179
00:16:26,520 --> 00:16:33,560
“He lies!” said my convict, with fierce energy. “He’s a liar born, and he’ll die a liar. Look at

180
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:40,359
his face; ain’t it written there? Let him turn those eyes of his on me. I defy him to do it.”

181
00:16:40,359 --> 00:16:43,880
The other, with an effort at a scornful smile, which could not,

182
00:16:43,880 --> 00:16:49,359
however, collect the nervous working of his mouth into any set expression, looked at the soldiers,

183
00:16:49,359 --> 00:16:55,880
and looked about at the marshes and at the sky, but certainly did not look at the speaker.

184
00:16:55,880 --> 00:17:02,239
“Do you see him?” pursued my convict. “Do you see what a villain he is? Do you see those grovelling

185
00:17:02,239 --> 00:17:08,680
and wandering eyes? That’s how he looked when we were tried together. He never looked at me.”

186
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:13,279
The other, always working and working his dry lips and turning his eyes restlessly

187
00:17:13,279 --> 00:17:18,800
about him far and near, did at last turn them for a moment on the speaker, with the words,

188
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:25,159
“You are not much to look at,” and with a half-taunting glance at the bound hands. At

189
00:17:25,159 --> 00:17:30,600
that point, my convict became so frantically exasperated, that he would have rushed upon

190
00:17:30,600 --> 00:17:37,840
him but for the interposition of the soldiers. “Didn’t I tell you,” said the other convict then,

191
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:43,439
“that he would murder me, if he could?” And any one could see that he shook with fear,

192
00:17:43,439 --> 00:17:49,199
and that there broke out upon his lips curious white flakes, like thin snow.

193
00:17:49,199 --> 00:17:53,960
“Enough of this parley,” said the sergeant. “Light those torches.”

194
00:17:53,960 --> 00:17:59,720
As one of the soldiers, who carried a basket in lieu of a gun, went down on his knee to open it,

195
00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:06,079
my convict looked round him for the first time, and saw me. I had alighted from Joe’s back on

196
00:18:06,079 --> 00:18:11,720
the brink of the ditch when we came up, and had not moved since. I looked at him eagerly

197
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:17,960
when he looked at me, and slightly moved my hands and shook my head. I had been waiting

198
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:22,920
for him to see me that I might try to assure him of my innocence. It was not at all expressed to

199
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:28,319
me that he even comprehended my intention, for he gave me a look that I did not understand,

200
00:18:28,319 --> 00:18:33,880
and it all passed in a moment. But if he had looked at me for an hour or for a day,

201
00:18:33,880 --> 00:18:39,600
I could not have remembered his face ever afterwards, as having been more attentive.

202
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:45,439
The soldier with the basket soon got a light, and lighted three or four torches, and took one

203
00:18:45,439 --> 00:18:52,119
himself and distributed the others. It had been almost dark before, but now it seemed quite dark,

204
00:18:52,119 --> 00:18:59,119
and soon afterwards very dark. Before we departed from that spot, four soldiers standing in a ring,

205
00:18:59,119 --> 00:19:05,319
fired twice into the air. Presently we saw other torches kindled at some distance behind us, and

206
00:19:05,319 --> 00:19:12,640
others on the marshes on the opposite bank of the river. “All right,” said the sergeant. “March.”

207
00:19:12,640 --> 00:19:17,039
We had not gone far when three cannon were fired ahead of us with a sound that seemed to burst

208
00:19:17,039 --> 00:19:23,319
something inside my ear. “You are expected on board,” said the sergeant to my convict;

209
00:19:23,319 --> 00:19:28,840
“they know you are coming. Don’t straggle, my man. Close up here.”

210
00:19:28,840 --> 00:19:34,560
The two were kept apart, and each walked surrounded by a separate guard. I had

211
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:41,119
hold of Joe’s hand now, and Joe carried one of the torches. Mr. Wopsle had been for going back, but

212
00:19:41,119 --> 00:19:47,640
Joe was resolved to see it out, so we went on with the party. There was a reasonably good path now,

213
00:19:47,640 --> 00:19:52,640
mostly on the edge of the river, with a divergence here and there where a dike came, with a miniature

214
00:19:52,640 --> 00:19:58,520
windmill on it and a muddy sluice-gate. When I looked round, I could see the other lights coming

215
00:19:58,520 --> 00:20:05,279
in after us. The torches we carried dropped great blotches of fire upon the track, and I could see

216
00:20:05,279 --> 00:20:12,640
those, too, lying smoking and flaring. I could see nothing else but black darkness. Our lights

217
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:18,840
warmed the air about us with their pitchy blaze, and the two prisoners seemed rather to like that,

218
00:20:18,840 --> 00:20:24,479
as they limped along in the midst of the muskets. We could not go fast, because of their lameness;

219
00:20:24,479 --> 00:20:29,720
and they were so spent, that two or three times we had to halt while they rested.

220
00:20:29,720 --> 00:20:35,640
After an hour or so of this travelling, we came to a rough wooden hut and a landing-place. There

221
00:20:35,640 --> 00:20:42,399
was a guard in the hut, and they challenged, and the sergeant answered. Then, we went into the hut,

222
00:20:42,399 --> 00:20:48,279
where there was a smell of tobacco and whitewash, and a bright fire, and a lamp, and a stand of

223
00:20:48,279 --> 00:20:55,359
muskets, and a drum, and a low wooden bedstead, like an overgrown mangle without the machinery,

224
00:20:55,359 --> 00:21:01,680
capable of holding about a dozen soldiers all at once. Three or four soldiers who lay upon it

225
00:21:01,680 --> 00:21:07,199
in their great-coats were not much interested in us, but just lifted their heads and took a sleepy

226
00:21:07,199 --> 00:21:14,800
stare, and then lay down again. The sergeant made some kind of report, and some entry in a book, and

227
00:21:14,800 --> 00:21:22,319
then the convict whom I call the other convict was drafted off with his guard, to go on board first.

228
00:21:22,319 --> 00:21:28,800
My convict never looked at me, except that once. While we stood in the hut,

229
00:21:28,800 --> 00:21:34,319
he stood before the fire looking thoughtfully at it, or putting up his feet by turns upon the hob,

230
00:21:35,199 --> 00:21:38,560
and looking thoughtfully at them as if he pitied them for their recent

231
00:21:38,560 --> 00:21:44,399
adventures. Suddenly, he turned to the sergeant, and remarked,—

232
00:21:44,399 --> 00:21:46,279
“I wish to say something respecting this

233
00:21:46,279 --> 00:21:51,079
escape. It may prevent some persons laying under suspicion alonger me.”

234
00:21:51,079 --> 00:21:55,720
“You can say what you like,” returned the sergeant, standing coolly looking at him

235
00:21:55,720 --> 00:22:00,279
with his arms folded, “but you have no call to say it here. You’ll have

236
00:22:00,279 --> 00:22:05,560
opportunity enough to say about it, and hear about it, before it’s done with, you know.”

237
00:22:05,560 --> 00:22:11,439
“I know, but this is another pint, a separate matter. A man can’t starve;

238
00:22:11,439 --> 00:22:14,279
at least I can’t. I took some wittles,

239
00:22:14,279 --> 00:22:19,479
up at the willage over yonder,—where the church stands a’most out on the marshes.”

240
00:22:19,479 --> 00:22:21,680
“You mean stole,” said the sergeant.

241
00:22:21,680 --> 00:22:24,600
“And I’ll tell you where from. From the blacksmith’s.”

242
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:27,640
“Halloa!” said the sergeant, staring at Joe.

243
00:22:27,640 --> 00:22:30,239
“Halloa, Pip!” said Joe, staring at me.

244
00:22:30,239 --> 00:22:36,520
“It was some broken wittles—that’s what it was—and a dram of liquor, and a pie.”

245
00:22:36,520 --> 00:22:38,840
“Have you happened to miss such an article as a pie,

246
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:41,920
blacksmith?” asked the sergeant, confidentially.

247
00:22:41,920 --> 00:22:47,159
“My wife did, at the very moment when you came in. Don’t you know, Pip?”

248
00:22:47,159 --> 00:22:52,680
“So,” said my convict, turning his eyes on Joe in a moody manner, and without the least glance

249
00:22:52,680 --> 00:22:59,640
at me,—“so you’re the blacksmith, are you? Than I’m sorry to say, I’ve eat your pie.”

250
00:22:59,640 --> 00:23:04,279
“God knows you’re welcome to it,—so far as it was ever mine,” returned Joe,

251
00:23:04,279 --> 00:23:09,159
with a saving remembrance of Mrs. Joe. “We don’t know what you have done,

252
00:23:09,159 --> 00:23:15,760
but we wouldn’t have you starved to death for it, poor miserable fellow-creatur.—Would us, Pip?”

253
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:20,920
The something that I had noticed before, clicked in the man’s throat again, and he turned his back.

254
00:23:20,920 --> 00:23:25,760
The boat had returned, and his guard were ready, so we followed him to the landing-place made of

255
00:23:25,760 --> 00:23:31,600
rough stakes and stones, and saw him put into the boat, which was rowed by a crew of convicts

256
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:38,159
like himself. No one seemed surprised to see him, or interested in seeing him, or glad to see him,

257
00:23:38,159 --> 00:23:44,199
or sorry to see him, or spoke a word, except that somebody in the boat growled as if to dogs,

258
00:23:44,199 --> 00:23:50,479
“Give way, you!” which was the signal for the dip of the oars. By the light of the torches,

259
00:23:50,479 --> 00:23:56,800
we saw the black Hulk lying out a little way from the mud of the shore, like a wicked Noah’s ark.

260
00:23:56,800 --> 00:24:02,159
Cribbed and barred and moored by massive rusty chains, the prison-ship seemed in my young eyes

261
00:24:02,159 --> 00:24:08,840
to be ironed like the prisoners. We saw the boat go alongside, and we saw him taken up the side and

262
00:24:08,840 --> 00:24:15,439
disappear. Then, the ends of the torches were flung hissing into the water, and went out,

263
00:24:15,439 --> 00:24:18,000
as if it were all over with him. Chapter 6.
